 # K-planes
+
 Also called: hex-planes, k-planes factorization, tri-planes
 
 ## Overview
-K-planes is a white-box, hybrid representation for volumetric rendering in arbitrary dimensions $d$ that factorizes the scene function into $k = \binom{d}{2}$ learned 2D feature planes [S2]. It solves the scalability and black-box nature of implicit neural fields by using explicit planar grids combined with a decoder, achieving high compression (e.g., 1000x over 4D grids) and fast optimization [S2]. Unlike sibling methods that add features from planes [S2], K-planes multiplies them element-wise, which enables spatial localization and allows the use of a linear decoder with a learned color basis instead of a non-linear MLP [S2].
+K-planes is a white-box, interpretable radiance field representation that factorizes a $d$-dimensional scene into $k = \binom{d}{2}$ orthogonal 2D feature planes [S2]. It solves the scalability and memory bottlenecks of implicit neural representations and high-dimensional voxel grids by using planar factorization to represent spatial and temporal interactions compactly [S2]. Unlike sibling methods such as [Forplane](5177-forplane.md) or [Feature Grid as Radiance Field](5508-feature-grid-as-radiance-field.md) which often rely on additive feature combination or complex hashing, K-planes uniquely employs element-wise multiplication (Hadamard product) of plane features to enforce spatial localization, enabling the use of a linear decoder for view-dependent color while maintaining competitive reconstruction quality [S2].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A query point $\mathbf{q} \in \mathbb{R}^d$ (typically spatial coordinates $\mathbf{x}$ and optionally viewing direction $\mathbf{d}$ or time $t$).
-*   **Output:** Density $\sigma \in \mathbb{R}$ and color $\mathbf{c} \in \mathbb{R}^3$.
+*   **Input:** A query point $\mathbf{q} \in \mathbb{R}^d$ (typically spatial coordinates $\mathbf{x}$, viewing direction $\mathbf{d}$, and optionally time $t$).
+*   **Output:** A signal vector containing density $\sigma$ and color $\mathbf{c}$ [G].
 
 **Data Structures:**
-The core data structure is a set of $k = \binom{d}{2}$ 2D feature planes $\mathbf{P} = \{ \mathbf{P}_c \}_{c \in C}$.
-*   **Plane Layout:** Each plane $\mathbf{P}_c$ has shape $N \times N \times M$.
